// Data of the theorem graph (theorems.html): theorems, lemmas, corollaries and conjectures of differential equations,
// MSC 2020 34 (ordinary differential equations), 35 (partial differential equations) and 58J (PDEs on manifolds, index
// theory, spectral geometry). In English. Built on 3 November 2026. Moved here by rule 1 of ../../CLAUDE.md, with its
// id: `picard-lindelof` from ../../analysis/ (there in the area "basic").
//
// Model as in ../../algebraic-number-theory/data/theorem-graph.js (see its header comment): `premises` and `proof` are
// the standard textbook proof, named in `proofName`; further proofs in `alternativeProofs`; `foundation: true` marks a
// proof that rests on theory outside these graphs (named in `proof`). `area` is the three-character MSC class, as in
// data/concepts.js; the theorems on Sobolev spaces (46E35) stand in the area "basic" until analysis takes them over.
// `concepts` are ids of data/concepts.js or carry the prefix of another folder.
// References to theorems of other folders: analysis "ana:" (fundamental theorem of calculus, Arzelà–Ascoli, dominated
// convergence, Riesz representation, spectral theorem for compact operators, …), topology "top:" (Banach fixed-point
// theorem, Jordan curve theorem, Stokes' theorem, Heine–Borel, Baire), geometry "geo:" (isoperimetric inequality);
// planned folders: linear algebra "la:" (Jordan normal form) and K-theory "kt:" (Bott periodicity).
// Concepts of the planned folders dynamical systems "ds:" (flow, ω-limit set, stability, Lyapunov function, limit
// cycle) and calculus of variations "cv:" (Dirichlet energy, Rayleigh quotient) are used in `concepts`.
//
// Sources: Walter, Ordinary Differential Equations (1998); Hartman, Ordinary Differential Equations (1964); Hirsch,
// Smale and Devaney (2004); Evans, Partial Differential Equations (2nd ed. 2010); Gilbarg and Trudinger, Elliptic
// Partial Differential Equations of Second Order (1983); John, Partial Differential Equations (4th ed. 1982); Courant
// and Hilbert, Methods of Mathematical Physics I–II; Lawson and Michelsohn, Spin Geometry (1989); Berline, Getzler and
// Vergne, Heat Kernels and Dirac Operators (1992); Fefferman, Existence and smoothness of the Navier–Stokes equation
// (Clay problem description, 2000). Not checked by a specialist.
// Checked by ../../tests/check-theorems.js.
// `fromDefinitions: true` marks a theorem whose usual proof uses only definitions and axioms of its area and no
// theorem (it has no premises); a theorem without premises that rests on theory outside these graphs carries
// `foundation: true`, and its `proof` names that theory.

